  "evidence": "TEXT_F: NHS Patient’s Guarantee with choice if waits are breached; restore ward sister/matron authority; hospice funding and an Exceptional Medicines Fund; tougher drug laws (especially near schools) and protect children online; strengthen victims’ rights and clarify self‑defence; emphasis on national identity, keep the pound, NATO/independent deterrent, and secure asylum reception centres; Blue Belts for waterways and protect school playing fields; extensive family support (Children’s Tax Credit rise, Married Couple’s Allowance, carers, widowed parents). TEXT_C: Leave the EU, restore full sovereignty, keep the pound, oppose EU army/constitution, and enact a UK Bill of Rights; strict GMO labelling, support for organics and high animal welfare, conservation zones and bans on damaging fishing methods, and local health‑focused monitoring; more police, visible community policing, deterrent sentencing and ‘life means life’; NHS decentralisation, nurse pay rises, +£2bn funding, recruit globally; immigration policy solely by UK, skills‑based admissions, faster fair but firm asylum with secure reception; education selection/streaming and protect school sports; benefits so work always pays and consider basic income for vulnerable groups; regain exclusive fisheries control and support rural communities via Land Management Contracts.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts engage Care and Authority, but TEXT_C adds a stronger Sanctity/Purity appeal (naturalness, GMO labelling, organics, animal welfare, conservation) and a more explicit Loyalty/patriotism stance (full sovereignty, border control) than TEXT_F. Given the electorate’s top priorities of Care and Sanctity, with notable weight on Authority and Loyalty, TEXT_C aligns slightly better overall despite TEXT_F’s robust care provisions.",
